• Yes—your desired transaction lifecycle is possible, and in my opinion it better matches the real cryptographic lifecycle of these addresses.

  No files were changed, and nothing was committed or pushed.

  ## What the wallet does now

  The current state machine is:

  FRESH → PENDING → SPENT → RETIRED
          receiving   spending

  More precisely:

  - FRESH means generated and available in the address pool.
  - Clicking “Mark Payment Received” changes it to PENDING.
  - Transaction signing accepts only a PENDING source address.
  - After the spending transaction confirms, OnConfirmation changes it to SPENT.
  - Optional manual key destruction later changes it to RETIRED.

  This is enforced in several places:

  - wallet/wallet.go:189 implements MarkPaymentReceived.
  - wallet/wallet.go:597 rejects signing unless the source is PENDING.
  - keystore/keystore.go:251 permits only FRESH → PENDING.
  - keystore/keystore.go:257 permits only PENDING → SPENT.
  - cmd/gui/main.go:360 includes only PENDING addresses in the From dropdown.

  The manual button is therefore not merely a GUI inconvenience. It is currently the operation that makes an address eligible to sign.

  ## The conceptual problem

  PENDING currently combines multiple unrelated meanings:

  - A payment was received.
  - The address should no longer be returned by NextReceiveAddress.
  - The address is eligible to spend.
  - A change address has been assigned to a signed transaction.
  - The address is awaiting confirmation.

  Those are different lifecycle events.

  Receiving coins does not expose the public key. The address commitment remains HASH256(public key), so a funded address can remain cryptographically unexposed for months or years. The public key is revealed only when the spending transaction is broadcast in its witness.

  Consequently, there is no cryptographic reason to require the address to be marked PENDING immediately after receiving coins. That requirement is wallet bookkeeping, not a Qogecoin consensus requirement.

  ## Recommended lifecycle

  I would not implement this by merely changing the signer from:

  allow PENDING

  to:

  allow FRESH

  That would bypass the existing single-use protection and could permit multiple signed transactions under the same key.

  A safer lifecycle would be approximately:

  AVAILABLE/UNFUNDED
          ↓ address assigned or payment detected
  ACTIVE/FUNDED, key never revealed
          ↓ successful transaction signing/broadcast reservation
  SPEND_PENDING
          ↓ spending transaction confirmed
  SPENT
          ↓ optional manual key purge after 101+ confirmations
  RETIRED

  Under that model:

  - A funded address can remain unspent and key-hidden indefinitely.
  - No “Mark Payment Received” action is necessary to unlock spending.
  - The wallet determines spendability from live UTXOs, not from a manually set receipt flag.
  - Once a transaction has been signed, the source becomes SPEND_PENDING so it cannot sign an unrelated second spend.
  - Once the transaction confirms, it becomes SPENT.
  - Key purging remains a separate optional action.

  If you want to retain the visible name FRESH, it could mean “has never revealed its key,” but the wallet would still need separate metadata indicating whether the address is available for assignment, assigned/funded, or reserved by a signed spend.

  ## Why a separate pending-spend state matters

  Your idea of marking it SPENT only after it really spends is correct if “really spends” means the transaction has confirmed.

  However, the wallet also needs protection between signing and confirmation:

  signed → manually broadcast → mempool → confirmed

  The current GUI does not broadcast automatically; it produces raw transaction hex for manual broadcasting. That is visible at cmd/gui/main.go:496.

  During that interval, the wallet must remember that the address has already signed a transaction. Otherwise the user could accidentally create another transaction using the same source/key. Therefore:

  - After successful signing: SPEND_PENDING.
  - After confirmation: SPENT.

  It should generally be automatic rather than requiring the user to press a “Mark Spent” button.

  ## Change addresses also need redesign consideration

  The current signer changes the new change address from FRESH → PENDING immediately after signing:

  wallet/wallet.go:681

  This was added to prevent the same change address from being selected again. That security goal is valid, but PENDING is again overloaded. The change address has not exposed its public key and has not spent anything—it has merely been reserved as an output.

  A cleaner design would reserve the change address separately, then regard it as active/funded once the transaction is seen or confirmed.

  ## Other transaction limitations I found

  The GUI currently has two additional constraints relevant to the workflow you described:

  - The recipient dropdown is populated only with FRESH addresses belonging to this wallet. It does not presently appear to accept an arbitrary exchange or another user’s address. See cmd/gui/main.go:376.
  - The send flow supports exactly one UTXO at the source address. If an address has received multiple deposits, it refuses to construct the transaction. See cmd/gui/main.go:429.

  So supporting the full workflow—sending part of a long-held balance to an external address—will require more than removing the “Mark Payment Received” button.

  ## Conclusion

  The change is absolutely implementable without changing Qogecoin consensus or the SLH-DSA transaction format. It is principally a Symbiont-wallet state-model and GUI redesign.

  My recommendation is:

  - Keep funded, never-spent addresses key-unrevealed and spendable indefinitely.
  - Remove manual receipt-marking as a prerequisite for spending.
  - Derive spendability from confirmed live UTXOs.
  - Introduce an explicit spend-reservation/pending state at successful signing or broadcast.
  - Mark the source SPENT when its spending transaction confirms.
  - Track address assignment/change reservation separately from public-key exposure.
  - Allow arbitrary valid external recipient addresses.
  - Eventually support selecting multiple UTXOs.

  The existing design has strong single-use safeguards, so the implementation should preserve those while correcting what the states mean. A small condition change would work superficially; a modest state-machine redesign would be the correct solution.

